# Host crontab for the two scheduled behaviours the behaviour spec requires (ADR-0007,
# spec §5 follow-up cadence, spec §10 audit-sample maintenance), run as
# `docker compose run --rm worker --job=<name>` — the same image and `--job` entrypoint the
# Container Apps Jobs design specified (ADR-0007: "Container Apps Jobs become cron"), so no
# application code differs between this deployment and the managed target state.
#
# Install (as the deploy user, from the repo checkout on the VM, e.g. /opt/erria):
#   crontab deploy/crontab
#
# Times are 5 minutes apart purely to avoid two `docker compose run` invocations racing the
# same Postgres connection pool on a single-vCPU-burst box at once — the behaviour spec does
# not mandate an exact time of day for either job, and both job bodies are stubs today (see
# apps/worker/src/jobs/run-job.ts), so there is no cadence-sensitive behaviour yet to tune
# this against. Revisit once #40/Plans 2-3 land the real job bodies.
SHELL=/bin/sh
# `&&`, not `;` — a job that exits non-zero (main.ts's catch-all sets that on any thrown error)
# must not publish a completion heartbeat. The heartbeat is a presence signal for "this job
# finished," not a magnitude for "this job succeeded some other way" — issue #62's alerting gaps.
0 6 * * * cd /opt/erria && docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.deploy.yaml run --rm worker --job=followup-cadence >> /var/log/erria/followup-cadence.log 2>&1 && deploy/scripts/report-job-heartbeat.sh "Followup Cadence Heartbeat" >> /var/log/erria/followup-cadence-heartbeat.log 2>&1
5 6 * * * cd /opt/erria && docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.deploy.yaml run --rm worker --job=audit-sample-maintenance >> /var/log/erria/audit-sample-maintenance.log 2>&1 && deploy/scripts/report-job-heartbeat.sh "Audit Sample Maintenance Heartbeat" >> /var/log/erria/audit-sample-maintenance-heartbeat.log 2>&1

# Monitoring (issue #61) — both scripts publish a custom Azure Monitor metric via the VM's own
# managed identity (deploy/scripts/lib-azure-metric.sh); infra/terraform/monitoring.tf alerts on
# them. Disk usage every 5 minutes: cheap to check, and the fastest-moving of the two — a log
# loop or a stray large file can fill 64 GiB in minutes, not days. TLS expiry once a day is
# plenty against a 90-day Let's Encrypt certificate; it needs DEPLOY_DOMAIN from .env, which the
# other two jobs above don't (docker compose reads .env itself; a plain script does not).
#
# `set -a` around the `.` is load-bearing on any line that sources .env for a *script* rather than
# for `docker compose`: sourcing sets shell variables, and shell variables are not passed to child
# processes. Without it `report-tls-expiry.sh` sees no DEPLOY_DOMAIN and exits 1 on its own
# `${DEPLOY_DOMAIN:?}` guard every night — verified with `sh -c '. ./env && ./child.sh'`, which
# prints an empty value. (`docker compose` is unaffected: it reads .env itself.)
*/5 * * * * /opt/erria/deploy/scripts/report-disk-usage.sh >> /var/log/erria/disk-usage.log 2>&1
17 3 * * * set -a && . /opt/erria/.env && set +a && /opt/erria/deploy/scripts/report-tls-expiry.sh >> /var/log/erria/tls-expiry.log 2>&1

# Autonomous-send alerting (issue #62) — kill-switch state (so a flip can be alerted on),
# autonomous send volume (the anomaly tripwire), audit-sample review backlog, and estimated Claude
# API spend. Every 5 minutes, matching disk usage above: the kill switch and send volume are the
# fastest-moving of the four, and running all four together costs one extra `docker compose run`
# per tick, not four. See deploy/scripts/report-autonomous-alerting-metrics.sh for why this one
# needs the worker container (a database query) rather than a plain host script.
*/5 * * * * cd /opt/erria && deploy/scripts/report-autonomous-alerting-metrics.sh >> /var/log/erria/autonomous-alerting-metrics.log 2>&1

# Nightly pg_dump to Azure Blob Storage (issue #60) — the whole of this deployment's recovery
# story, since ADR-0007 traded away Flexible Server's continuous backup. Restore procedure:
# deploy/restore-runbook.md.
#
# 02:30 UTC, not alongside the 06:00/06:05 jobs above: the dump reads every table and the two
# worker jobs write, so the quietest hour of the night is worth having them not overlap, and
# nothing about this deployment is busy at 02:30 in either Denmark or Vietnam.
#
# `set -a` for the same reason as the TLS line above — backup-postgres.sh needs POSTGRES_USER /
# POSTGRES_DB / BACKUP_STORAGE_ACCOUNT exported, not merely set.
#
# `&&` before the heartbeat, exactly as the two worker jobs do: the heartbeat means "a dump was
# taken, verified and uploaded", so a failed run must publish none and let
# azurerm_monitor_metric_alert.postgres_backup_absent notice the silence. A failed run separately
# publishes its own "Postgres Backup Failure" metric from inside the script, which is the faster
# and more specific of the two alerts — see infra/terraform/monitoring.tf.
30 2 * * * cd /opt/erria && set -a && . ./.env && set +a && deploy/scripts/backup-postgres.sh >> /var/log/erria/backup-postgres.log 2>&1 && deploy/scripts/report-job-heartbeat.sh "Postgres Backup Heartbeat" >> /var/log/erria/backup-postgres-heartbeat.log 2>&1
